For each index n, the principal element generated by the n-th neighbourhood of a computable presentation is computable.

Pairing the fixed index n with a variable index is primitive recursive, and composing the presentation's decidable inclusion relation with that pairing decides inclusion of the n-th neighbourhood in the m-th. Membership of the m-th neighbourhood in the principal element is that inclusion together with the set being a neighbourhood, which the presentation provides. The resulting decidable predicate is recursively enumerable.
